        solution:
          "Output: `[1 2 3 100 5] [2 3 100]`. `b` shares `a`'s backing array with **len 2 and cap 4** (capacity runs from index 1 to the end of `a`). Because there is spare capacity, `append` does not allocate – it writes 100 into the next slot of the shared array, which is `a[3]`. This aliasing is one of the most common slice bugs in production code. To make `b` independent, use `slices.Clone(a[1:3])`, `copy` into a new slice, or the full slice expression `a[1:3:3]`, which limits the capacity so the append is forced to allocate a new array.",
