        solution:
          "`context.WithValue` is intended for request‑scoped data that crosses API boundaries (e.g., trace ID, authentication token, request start time). It should not be used for optional function parameters or business logic configuration. **Best practices**: Use private key types (not built‑in string keys) to prevent collisions. Do not store large objects; context is immutable so a new value creates a new context wrapper. Never use context as a general purpose map. Only store data that is truly request‑scoped and needed by many components. Loggers sometimes accept context to extract trace IDs. The pros: no thread‑local storage, explicitly passed. The cons: opaque, can hide dependencies. If you find yourself passing values everywhere, consider whether they could be part of explicit parameters instead.",
